170 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/P 122 0 R/T(When Walt Disney was laying the foundation for Disneyland in the 50s, critics thought he was crazy thinking that his cartoon theme park would get anywhere. Now, the Disney theme parks are the most successful in the industry, fulfilling Disney's vision of becoming the blueprint for amusement parks in the future. Still, a lot goes into making sure "The Happiest Place on Earth" is running smoothly. Workers of all kinds of backgrounds are needed, like: cast members to interact with the guests, imagineers to make sure everything is safe, ride operators, food and merchandising staff, and so much more. Additionally, population control is a huge part of the success at the Disney Parks. Rides or experiences that have the capacity for lots of riders, along with a long wait for these rides are used strategically to help maintain population control in Disney. Experiences like the Monsters Inc. Laugh Floor has the capacity for lots of guests, making walkways near this attraction easier to navigate. Lastly, you need fun attractions to keep the guests coming back. Rise of the Resistance, for example, is a fan favorite, and also happens to have the most concrete poured for a Disney ride, AND is a trackless thrill ride, giving riders a smoother experience.)/E(When Walt Disney was laying the foundation for Disneyland in the 50s, critics thought he was crazy thinking that his cartoon theme park would get anywhere. Now, the Disney theme parks are the most successful in the industry, fulfilling Disney's vision of becoming the blueprint for amusement parks in the future. Still, a lot goes into making sure "The Happiest Place on Earth" is running smoothly. Workers of all kinds of backgrounds are needed, like: cast members to interact with the guests, imagineers to make sure everything is safe, ride operators, food and merchandising staff, and so much more. Additionally, population control is a huge part of the success at the Disney Parks. Rides or experiences that have the capacity for lots of riders, along with a long wait for these rides are used strategically to help maintain population control in Disney. Experiences like the Monsters Inc. Laugh Floor has the capacity for lots of guests, making walkways near this attraction easier to navigate. Lastly, you need fun attractions to keep the guests coming back. Rise of the Resistance, for example, is a fan favorite, and also happens to have the most concrete poured for a Disney ride, AND is a trackless thrill ride, giving riders a smoother experience.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

